  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper studies feed-forward neurons as predictive features for hallucination detection, but does not test whether neuron poles have a single interpretable job, whether explanations generalize to held-out activations, or whether neuron-based attribution graphs explain model behaviour.",
    "gate_note": "The reported results concern classifier performance using selected neuron features, not the semantic interpretability or explanation quality named in the claim versions. The cited findings from prior studies are not recorded as results. Table I and Table III neuron-feature results are recorded as findings outside the listed versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study ranks feed-forward neurons by how well they separate truthful from hallucinated prompts, then reports how well hallucination classifiers trained on those neurons perform (AUROC). It never explains neurons or audits their jobs, scores explanations by simulation, builds attribution graphs, or compares architectures or tools for interpretability. Its results therefore cannot bear on any version. It neither cites the original sources nor relies on neurons being single-job; it only uses neuron activations as classifier features.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly correct. The authors say neurons chosen at the final prompt token can train hallucination classifiers on out-of-distribution factual questions, ranking among the top two methods by AUROC. In several cases they fall slightly below residual-stream MLP probes at the final generated token. The authors state that no causal role is established because no intervention was run.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper ranks neurons using class-conditioned residual-space contribution separation and evaluates downstream classifiers; it does not perform semantic neuron audits or test explanations on held-out activations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports that selected neurons concentrate in middle-to-late layers, that lower-layer-only performance is limited, and that depth curves plateau; the plotted depth results are not extracted as individual values here.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "About 1% of feed-forward neurons, chosen at the final prompt token on a CounterFact-based set, give hallucination classifiers on TriviaQA and TruthfulQA that rank among the top two methods. AUROC is about 0.72–0.84 for the MLP variant.",
        "proposed_claim": "A small set of feed-forward neurons, selected by truthful-versus-hallucinated mean separation on one dataset, carries hallucination-predictive signal that transfers to other closed-book QA datasets.",
